we could hardly compiled a list of  2015's best PC games without mentioning  The Witcher 3 Wild Hunt a title which  sets a new benchmark for fantasy action  rpgs fastness and longevity are not  always desirable qualities in their own  right but in this case the story of  supernatural contract killer Geralt of  Rivia spans more than a hundred hours  and so much of it will linger in your  memory your journey across the immense  northern kingdoms involves genuinely  interesting characters memorable  locations major plot decisions a fall on  your shoulders and awe-inspiring  monsters to free from the earth it's the  attention to detail the sharp writing  the vivid characters and the epic  heroics that make every hour enjoyable  we'll be foolish not to mention rocket  League as well as it's clearly the best  multiplayer game released this year this  is a soccer title with a perverse  ingenious twist the footballers have  been replaced by cars your shiny blocks  of metal and carbon fiber lack the  extremities required for nuanced ball  control yet you can go from zero to 60  in a couple of seconds the result is a  masterfully berserk depiction of the  beautiful game with players tumbling  across stadiums boosting their thrusters  skyward flipping and twisting their roll  cages with a bloodthirsty desire to  collide with a giant beach ball while  this chaotic spin on soccer seems like  an awful idea on paper in practice it's  an elegant masterpiece eliciting an  explosion of emotion in its players  Metal Gear Solid 5 the phantom pain  represents a change in direction that  Konami being the first in the series to  launch a day and date alongside the  console additions it also happens to be  the greatest stealth game of all time  replacing the series room by room level  structure with a vast stretch of Afghan  desert the freedom in your approach to  each mission adds a dazzling degree of  strategy and planning something that had  a natural limit in previous games the AI  which has always been excellent read the  series now has the freedom to be truly  spectacular with enemies reacting and  adapting to signs of your presence it's  by no means perfect the story's final  stretch being particularly divisive but  it's an extraordinary achievement  nonetheless  Paulette for represents another  remarkable achievement in a year packed  with acclaimed high-profile games even  for a Bethesda title the degree of  freedom offered to the player here is at  standing a whole library of side quests  can be discovered and pursued at your  convenience while instruments of death  in its bleak world can be crafted and  modified to your heart's content  dig deeper and you'll discover further  options to strengthen your armor mix  your own cans and pick out your  favorites from an extensive menu of  imaginative ability perks and then  there's the option to build entire  structures with their own electrical  circuits and farms forage from materials  you scavenged along the way fallout 4 is  fundamentally an arcade game thrusting  you into its world with little guidance  or the slick and mollycoddling of modern  triple-a games what it offers instead is  a promise that every moment of curiosity  and every creative thought you conjure  in this world will be handsomely  rewarded the fifth entrance on our list  is Kerbal space program a game that  gives you the keys to space and lets you  pick the destination from the  nail-biting first man launch to putting  the final touches on your multi module  refueling station in orbit around the  moon Kerbal space program strikes a  wonderful balance between education and  trial and error gameplay the semi real  physics model nails that sweet spot of  complexity making it accessible to  anyone with the desire to learn yet  avoiding the headache that can come with  an extremely realistic simulation when  weeks of iteration finally carries your  adorable Kerbal into the depths of space  you'll certainly feel like a rocket  scientist and when your moon landing is  just so slightly off tipping your craft  and dooming your poor kerbals you learn  another important lesson to never give  up  you
 

